#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134864} :activity/id #uuid "80a63c78-ab52-5147-b1df-b7736290c5a2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050567 :topic/id #uuid "54747772-1cac-572e-9b8f-be3a6c8abe45" :topic/math-academy-id 2198} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134864 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135131 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf F =P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ and a region $D$ is bounded by a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve $C,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/fa/fa3efbc9200a6acdda01e503999f7588cd44fd63af77fa0a6abf1b5303c9a889.png)\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now consider what happens when the scalar curl term on the left-hand side of the above equation equals unity everywhere on $D.$ In other words, let's consider what happens when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the double integral gives the area of $D,$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=\\text{Area}(D).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThus, we've just discovered a new method of computing the area of $D.$ We simply choose a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D,$ and then compute its circulation over $C.$\n\nThere are infinitely many vector fields that we can use to compute the area of a region. Some of the most common for this purpose are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)= \\left\\langle -y, \\: 0 \\right\\rangle, \n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)= \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{y}{2}, \\: \\dfrac{x}{2} \\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y)= \\left\\langle 0, \\: x \\right\\rangle\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bbaebc78-d8b2-5500-9c52-f82bd3d3a8a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5839 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "25733548-0d69-5314-abb7-7cf1ba40a7bc" :step/math-academy-id 35784 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134868}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134865 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133895 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133711 :question/id #uuid "a817c3c6-97bb-55e4-9a1e-04ed99d3eaab" :question/math-academy-id "e-9553"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f4f726e-d985-5915-a4d0-30cfa6f4b89b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051070}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561063}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Satisfy the Unit Curl Property"} :step/id #uuid "31e81ee5-877c-5590-999c-e1a635893093" :step/math-academy-id 35785 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134866}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134866 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133896 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133712 :question/id #uuid "74c9ecfb-5005-53ff-b7a9-db798488587b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9554"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8644454b-ee25-59fd-ae65-9770a0b44442" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050666}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523014} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522984}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Bounded by a Closed Curve Using Green’s Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "3ff52bb4-279b-55a3-9202-9e4939a84e2e" :step/math-academy-id 35786 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134867}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134867 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133897 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133713 :question/id #uuid "aa4400ff-09b0-596c-9857-c13881b72f73" :question/math-academy-id "e-9555"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540319} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561027}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "0aca8ded-b498-5e7b-a4b3-06277e60cbf3" :step/math-academy-id 35787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134868 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135132 :tutorial/content "As a concrete example, let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C,$ parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle 2\\cos t, \\: \\sin t \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in \\left [0, 2\\pi \\right).\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its positively oriented bounding curve $C$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6d9fe9cad4ccee56d5d61457c475b41371e0705f1aeccd3a18fb4a3fa8e127c.png)\n\nTo calculate the area of $D$ using Green's theorem, we need to find a vector field whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D.$ So, let's choose\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = 0.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0 - \\left(-1\\right) = 1. \\qquad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $C$ is a positively oriented path, the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting our expressions for $P$ and $Q$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = -\\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now evaluate the integral using the usual methods. The derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r'}(t) = \\langle -2\\sin t, \\: \\cos t \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo we can calculate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D)  =2\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f13baac9-fbd8-5a28-b168-f3010a604b1b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5840 :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"} :step/id #uuid "f6584994-83e5-5672-aa98-e6e1491a031c" :step/math-academy-id 35792 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134876}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134876 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135137 :tutorial/content "We've established that if a region $D$ is bounded by a positively oriented curve $C,$ and we select a vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ such that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} =  1,\n$$\n\nthen the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) =  \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNow, if $C$ is *negatively* oriented, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) =  -\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = -\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5c61d2a3-2e54-5e8b-931c-beff07f3581e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5857 :tutorial/title "Negatively Oriented Curves"} :step/id #uuid "2f9c02ea-f6d5-5e40-8f6b-2625f2260d4c" :step/math-academy-id 35869 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134865}}] :activity/title "Using Green's Theorem to Calculate Area" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134040}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442254} :activity/id #uuid "5a146ad8-7f02-557a-a03b-2c457ab4ba04" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050567 :topic/id #uuid "54747772-1cac-572e-9b8f-be3a6c8abe45" :topic/math-academy-id 2198} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442253 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "536ba388-fdb8-58ea-b126-38fbd2dcdb6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442233} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f93b2c-abf4-5cbd-aa5b-d281bc9dc445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442239} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5cc6be-612b-5620-9ea1-537324d438b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442240} {:answer/id #uuid "c3dbd51c-9d36-5509-bb8b-877222cbaef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442242} {:answer/id #uuid "de993e24-618b-5743-81ef-393206c8a109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442243}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442243} :answer-field/id #uuid "53872032-3b68-54a9-b047-b5722ee1ce8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "19d2b758-b269-5c92-aaaf-707449daf876" :question/math-academy-id "q-42301" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac9f6016ee4c93aec58f8fff0a8002057f89d7c1bae589e5357a25709ae5a76c.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-y\\,dx & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "26451fe3-7951-51a8-9a1b-fd3d8660fd3b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442256}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442254 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86f48c0b-2a3d-5163-b8e8-0c7233eb05fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132356} {:answer/id #uuid "e01d132b-6218-5a49-8b31-e23dbbbfbe93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132357} {:answer/id #uuid "ed54db10-308f-5357-92ca-c739526b9c08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132358} {:answer/id #uuid "fab5eb83-c4a0-5d86-82d7-ebea1c4f60b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132359} {:answer/id #uuid "6d37aff8-08ac-5983-b3ea-04ddf202a770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132360}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186132356} :answer-field/id #uuid "30db4754-f10f-5c4e-a207-166a2bda9bd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c299dba6-b9c4-559b-b05a-803d3465ef0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-42298" :question/problem "![](images/01/01a5f06674cacca4764f438a8c06f020659f0177a0b74952f40bc9072627ec3f.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=2{t}^{2}\\,i-{t}^{3}\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=4t\\,i-3{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-{t}^{3})\\cdot 4t\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3efe2d38-47eb-5856-81a2-431378bd1194" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442253}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442255 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079819d9-6be2-54b5-a40a-e93e9bbceca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442226} {:answer/id #uuid "30812918-b09d-5eac-98bf-46dd91f25063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442229} {:answer/id #uuid "6baabb12-e16e-5f6d-acae-67851198dc97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442234} {:answer/id #uuid "9beda9f3-8b47-5843-a2c9-b0565509b47e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442237} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e358f5-54ad-55b9-afe1-8f1e0f017b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442238}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442238} :answer-field/id #uuid "589fe02c-92ec-567f-8354-84271d506420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55130439-a2e5-560a-bbed-4ce47334253d" :question/math-academy-id "q-126757" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef44f01fb462cdf608f66a60dd11b8df798603bd204dca9d579ed50b370a84e3.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡2t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7f777294-56ab-5734-acea-b53e1763dfff"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442256 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f00612-ca24-58a9-9a99-5bc9dff70695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{sin}^{3}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442225} {:answer/id #uuid "244e3377-d03b-59bc-9a15-821691749cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442228} {:answer/id #uuid "3b19e5e7-17b2-5ecd-8bda-2f5d049ec56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442230} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac2fdfe-d325-50d4-8f82-69274f9797ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{sin}^{3}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442231} {:answer/id #uuid "4c93679f-96a9-5546-b831-28f2a59ed0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{sin}^{3}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442228} :answer-field/id #uuid "1007d99f-4a44-5eca-8672-e3953d3b25aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30739161-7e72-5e37-b131-9975b77440f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-42315" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bfec7cbdb26a3db773ab1f6207de436ff0856b331cda183da530dbb6b91d91bd.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-y\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-{sin}^{3}⁡t)\\cdot (-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ad8a8662-52fd-53e4-8d4b-b923614bdc82" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442257}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442257 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14451dd1-51ba-5cb4-a62a-0a5d4ff055bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{sin}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442227} {:answer/id #uuid "6f80c7b4-33fd-57db-876b-025e20021ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442235} {:answer/id #uuid "81dd9c9a-88ed-5edc-bb4a-ab112606e4e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442236} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4d50f5-796c-5fc2-a622-14130e42fc0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442241} {:answer/id #uuid "e911610a-7c47-5303-9138-bd0e5e56ae76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9{sin}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442244}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442235} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a72b7a-4d2b-5cb1-bfa9-dcf075591732" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6653118e-2998-5812-b728-f3217528d405" :question/math-academy-id "q-126746" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a60729b626e1e9e219a839898cacc18aa81ec2a9b1b445e7b92b721874c2550.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }9{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "db95ffdd-3589-5fdd-806c-5051d2294931" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442255}}] :activity/title "Using Green's Theorem to Calculate Area" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442224}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511633} :activity/id #uuid "abf9c4c3-4981-52ee-b589-3b1c3030a4cf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050567 :topic/id #uuid "54747772-1cac-572e-9b8f-be3a6c8abe45" :topic/math-academy-id 2198} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fe86c01-fa33-54c0-919e-aaad045de4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511016} {:answer/id #uuid "91cc57eb-09e7-59eb-aa0f-280a8c492891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511083} {:answer/id #uuid "94960e82-187d-589c-a1c2-ed096c94c497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511087} {:answer/id #uuid "bbaa8abc-d95e-51ca-a4d1-7a1aa516b455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511168} {:answer/id #uuid "f46a71c6-0e54-5d62-9732-93eefd0150bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511168} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdfd2c93-cc42-5e18-b039-6099f904aa29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "053eddb0-5c93-560e-b92c-a89951cbd49c" :question/math-academy-id "q-42239" :question/problem "![](images/93/938f91220033a2174c011c1f8d65ff61e81e79b8af0ef718411169364c885418.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=t\\,i+\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{t}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0]-[-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0fd57101-031a-56f6-9993-cefb5de3fe06" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511648}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1997d82a-e8fd-5876-bff6-f2a494d2c16e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510841} {:answer/id #uuid "2e996338-8bd4-5f85-acba-798d27184bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510881} {:answer/id #uuid "91c650e3-c5a3-551b-acac-5cc206d90d87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511082} {:answer/id #uuid "a50bd914-811d-5009-9a3f-16c3d9e26a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511129} {:answer/id #uuid "e5105f35-40d7-5290-bc6b-8489218cf6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511263} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f782c02-0bd7-576d-a703-443eb8f872fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "097af826-94c0-5b59-ad65-7d59555aa9e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-42221" :question/problem "![](images/44/4408d1fcee1d168c6ab21ea53cf24845093bad9cc608299a36f0510c1636cc03.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t},\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "80c380fc-d04f-536e-bc60-f772fa1cc0f7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3242ed2a-ee0f-5b6e-92a0-da63b077c141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510888} {:answer/id #uuid "482ab86f-4fe2-571e-b0ca-93e2bd5d4903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510943} {:answer/id #uuid "a3031b19-d41c-5adf-a1c1-fb61f1fe36c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511124} {:answer/id #uuid "bb43d5ac-3266-5668-89fa-191462493dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511167} {:answer/id #uuid "e19c04bb-3f86-514f-ad0d-22da5d1ba873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511250}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511167} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b42e264-51ff-5675-8ac9-a399601959a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ad2198cd-84ab-52d9-a653-128de0924655" :question/math-academy-id "q-126754" :question/problem "![](images/86/86772e27f0a3226a608a1188fd8f58b9a8956f5f02fdbc5f9c2d29e2cef2a54c.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,dx & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡2t(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-18{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =6({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a3e46484-6e96-5940-b74e-e01b342a1b8e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65573630-2003-5f37-a3a0-d25c98f4401a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510996} {:answer/id #uuid "6e780ffa-2a00-5af3-b5f3-92f795812999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511012} {:answer/id #uuid "9dbf5317-be7f-5ed0-a334-2b540864d0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511112} {:answer/id #uuid "a5484851-2c51-5273-8db2-309d04de9b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511130} {:answer/id #uuid "d89e0626-3b35-5c77-81ab-f0b1350e2a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511226}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510996} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2eb735b-b5bd-5b47-b78f-f9e6cc0794cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fdacb1ae-ddf6-5106-9485-7c1e14bd18a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-42297" :question/problem "![](images/35/35b8ad6fd40daf95ba4f77288321d6a570e2df2944d056b9445e9ba4e36f97b7.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a5cc6a28-05b8-5f80-aaa2-4f93656a403b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511676}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02757ddb-6720-5404-868b-7b1166fb2c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510781} {:answer/id #uuid "083e8245-b859-5f0f-b493-6e1eb22d754b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510797} {:answer/id #uuid "1e791730-85c4-5ff7-ad54-6e399657153c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510851} {:answer/id #uuid "3c70ca77-ec27-57f8-b078-51030fa03414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510920} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d4bd2c-dd7d-5ebf-8ab9-0d013cef2b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511284}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511284} :answer-field/id #uuid "1557ad1a-a45b-592a-8609-24271802b200" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ae04e3b-017d-534f-b76c-ac600584e435" :question/math-academy-id "q-126568" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)=-y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=y$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=6{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "eae9ae58-73f8-56ab-9f1a-1264510bfad1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511647}}] :activity/title "Using Green's Theorem to Calculate Area" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510763}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561097} :activity/id #uuid "12a6cff2-46b1-567e-9f11-e66243bf7f66" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050567 :topic/id #uuid "54747772-1cac-572e-9b8f-be3a6c8abe45" :topic/math-academy-id 2198} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561066 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561018 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18fdc301-b9e6-5380-b576-f756ad837d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560422} {:answer/id #uuid "48d9075b-21bf-518c-85e4-b52a39ca48c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560525} {:answer/id #uuid "71000db5-1bfa-5f19-88d0-bfac331f25f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560605} {:answer/id #uuid "79fb9fa0-3c09-5639-8f51-38fc29793534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560625} {:answer/id #uuid "aee8fb2f-93bf-5e56-b3ef-5f91d5ed3421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560716}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560525} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b9b5f42-303d-5796-888d-e11c20c8cac2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7d76f5fb-ba28-5b32-904d-cbb78731c152" :question/math-academy-id "q-126570" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)=-\\frac{y}{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\frac{x}{3}$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{3}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{3},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{2}{3}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III does *not* give the required area. Notice that \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x-2y\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "0413025a-a6cd-5d63-8b51-14b775eb02ec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561091}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5caa2745-f258-5524-bce2-0cee34f7a871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560562} {:answer/id #uuid "7484861d-d825-59f3-b995-2fbd5e8a6934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560612} {:answer/id #uuid "81e94b1a-bd75-573d-86e7-53f24a85af34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560639} {:answer/id #uuid "8ccdd078-e1bf-5bbc-be52-fd411e22349d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560656} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcde265-3bb2-5d78-a137-ab8cf1e73dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560859}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560656} :answer-field/id #uuid "041132e8-077c-5837-a79f-29852017427b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f61d70b2-c968-54d9-b087-2ae80ef01b89" :question/math-academy-id "q-126561" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)={e}^{y}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=y$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=y$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x}-{e}^{y}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II does *not* give the required area.  Notice that \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{2}}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "5743bec9-9fc8-52ec-8926-6eb7cdd3fcc1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561066}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3670e0fc-6d65-5c69-a28a-c92456932444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560485} {:answer/id #uuid "39c3f228-f440-590e-ba1b-1c90c3c03432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560493} {:answer/id #uuid "7521cafa-fe34-5822-be40-921e53636206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560614} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca6978e-a4e1-5484-ba5d-364891771b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560684} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d3daaf-a309-5e5e-a768-d106755411ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560729}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560729} :answer-field/id #uuid "75e5af1d-ea30-5a37-83f7-6f329a1a3e3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8d510a6d-f96b-598f-8f2e-31efe6a6d012" :question/math-academy-id "q-126869" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5eb703c2812d82848bb5a1639c2840c4abf9318b988087e2216d9881137da72.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)={t}^{4}\\,i+{t}^{3}\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=\\,i-t\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{3}\\cdot 4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{6}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "69e681a0-9b8e-5a97-98d5-a5c36baee9a4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cd6483d-ff42-5dc9-bf60-d49b491388dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560464} {:answer/id #uuid "5000cc2e-e84a-572e-94b0-98de14def1b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560539} {:answer/id #uuid "683ef1d0-3a45-5daf-a0c7-cf2a0f6be250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560583} {:answer/id #uuid "be4e7b49-1ac4-5e2f-92d1-d5c217fc797f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560741} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5e01ff-4835-5778-92d5-2ea89a8203bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560799}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560464} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea3760f2-d10c-5617-a3f3-b8335b0f89d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2d6a46ea-e810-5ed6-9d15-12e3845c1e76" :question/math-academy-id "q-126572" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}-y,\\,x{e}^{y}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y},\\,0⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{5}+{x}^{7},\\,\\frac{6x}{5}⟩$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I give the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{e}^{y}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{5}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{6}{5},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{7}{5}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "badd52ef-742f-5ca8-9362-960bb5d0223a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561076}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "536431fa-077c-5c63-8e52-e720d5f25ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560546} {:answer/id #uuid "57095cd5-869a-52ea-98e5-ae49745a6cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560553} {:answer/id #uuid "58122a63-9d7f-591f-b933-aa93158e184f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560555} {:answer/id #uuid "629d2292-4c54-5f88-aab2-977463ff11a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560573} {:answer/id #uuid "88b89e38-2d3c-57a2-ad5a-0be2309918a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560646}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560573} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bca7dbd-ffdc-5cb7-822a-009c4eccd416" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f892a9d1-561b-51b8-a283-3b892f8dc787" :question/math-academy-id "q-126559" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)=y$ and $Q(x,y)=x$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)=y+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{3}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "dfd8a0a5-40c9-5766-af65-8e056002dbdd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561073}}] :activity/title "Using Green's Theorem to Calculate Area" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560352}]}
